item.description:
arXiv:2611.07290v1 Announce Type: new 
Abstract: For a fixed $k$-uniform hypergraph $F$, let $\mathrm{ex}_k(n,F)$ denote the maximum number of edges in an $F$-free $k$-uniform hypergraph on $n$ vertices. We prove that for every integer $k\ge2$ and every rational $r\in[0,1]$ there exists a $k$-uniform hypergraph $F_{k,r}$ such that
  \begin{equation*}
  \mathrm{ex}_k(n,F_{k,r})=\Theta(n^{k-r}).
  \end{equation*}
  The case $k=2$ is the rational exponents conjecture of Erd\H{o}s and Simonovits \cite{Erdos1988}, also known as Erd\H{o}s problem \#571 \cite{BloomErdoswebsite}; a proof was announced in September 2026 and is expounded by Conlon et al.\ \cite{Conlon-Janzer-Jiang-Kim-Lee-Liu-Longbrake-Yepremyan}. For $k\ge3$ the theorem extends their result to $k$-uniform hypergraphs. In the single-hypergraph setting, this strengthens Fitch's finite-family theorem \cite{Fitch} by realizing each exponent with a single forbidden $k$-uniform hypergraph for $0\le r\le1$. We also show, via an explicit two-edge construction, that for $k\ge4$ this strengthening fails for $\lceil k/2\rceil<r\le k-1$: no single $k$-uniform hypergraph $F$ then satisfies $\mathrm{ex}_k(n,F)=\Theta(n^{k-r})$. The main result of this paper has been formalized in Lean 4, conditional on two explicitly stated axioms.
